<!--
  Skeletal (fill-in) version of 4.6 Tangent Planes and Differentials.

  Definitions, theorems, summaries, and problem statements are kept;
  derivations and solutions are replaced by blank workspace boxes.  No
  hints (Nov 2026, at the instructor's request): no instruction lines
  say how to do a derivation or an example, no box row names a method,
  and no caption, figure label or alt text gives an answer away; a
  solution keeps at most a pointer to the figure that checks it.  Content
  mirrors
  TangentPlanesDifferentials.ptx of the full lecture notes; every xml:id
  here is prefixed with "skel-" so the two can coexist.

  Figures: every figure in this section is a surface, a prefigure
  schematic, or a zoomed plot that no student can draw by hand in class,
  so all of them are reproduced in full.  Each one sits AFTER the box
  whose work it illustrates, so it reads as a check rather than as the
  answer.  The video and the interactive tangent-plane builder are kept,
  the interactive gated to the web target with a print note beside it.

  The tangent plane is found from the cross product of the direction vectors
  of the two tangent lines, as in the full notes (Nov 2026).  The three
  derivations of the section (the tangent plane, the normal vector as a
  gradient, the estimate of Delta f) each get ONE empty workspace with a last
  row for the result: the text before it names what is derived, never how,
  and does not break the work into steps (instructor request, Nov 2026).
  The figures of
  that derivation, of the first example and of "Estimating the Change in a
  Particular Direction" are Asymptote (interactive in HTML, vector PDF in
  print); where the full notes' figure carries an answer in its labels (the
  components of a vector, the equation of a plane, a numerical value), the
  copy here leaves it out.  differential-estimate-manim.html, the interactive
  that compares df with Delta f, is gated to the web target as well.
-->
<section xml:id="sec-skel-fmv-tangent-planes" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Tangent Planes and Differentials</title>

<objectives>
<introduction>
<p>
After completing this section, you should be able to:
</p>
</introduction>

<ul>
<li>
<p>
Derive the equation of the tangent plane to the surface <m>z = f(x,y)</m>
at a point <m>P_0(x_0, y_0, f(x_0,y_0))</m> from the cross product of the
direction vectors of the two tangent lines in the planes <m>y = y_0</m>
and <m>x = x_0</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Find the normal vector <m>\mathbf n = \nabla w \Big|_{P_0}</m> of the
tangent plane, where <m>w(x,y,z) = f(x,y) - z</m>, and write the
parametric equations of the normal line to the surface at <m>P_0</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Estimate the change <m>\Delta f</m> in the value of <m>f</m> produced by
moving a small distance <m>ds</m> away from <m>P_0</m> in the direction
of a unit vector <m>\hat{u}</m>, using the differential
<m>df = \left(\nabla f \Big|_{P_0} \cdot \hat{u}\right) ds</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Find the linearization <m>L(x,y)</m> of a differentiable function at a
point and use the tangent plane as a linear approximation of the surface.
</p>
</li>
</ul>
</objectives>

<introduction>
<p>
Recall from <xref ref="sec-skel-fmv-partial-derivatives"/> that the
partial derivative <m>f_x(x_0,y_0)</m> is the slope of the tangent line
to the curve cut from the surface <m>z = f(x,y)</m> by the plane
<m>y = y_0</m>, and that <m>f_y(x_0,y_0)</m> is the slope of the tangent
line to the curve cut by the plane <m>x = x_0</m>. In this section we put
these two tangent lines together: the plane containing both of them is
the tangent plane to the surface at <m>P_0(x_0, y_0, f(x_0,y_0))</m>. We
find its equation, use its normal vector to write the equation of the
normal line to the surface, estimate the change in <m>f</m> caused by
moving a small distance away from <m>P_0</m> in a given direction, and
finally use the tangent plane to linearize <m>f</m> near a point.
</p>
</introduction>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="subsec-skel-tp-equation">
<title>Equation of the Tangent Plane</title>

<p>
We now derive the equation of the tangent plane to the surface
<m>z = f(x,y)</m> at <m>P_0(x_0, y_0, z_0)</m>, where
<m>z_0 = f(x_0,y_0)</m>.
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,15);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3.25) -- (15,3.25);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,14.75) {workspace};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,2.95) {the tangent plane:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace for the derivation, with a last row for the equation of the tangent plane.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<assemblage xml:id="skel-summary-tp-tangent-plane">
<title>The Tangent Plane</title>
<p>
The equation of the <term>tangent plane to the surface</term>
<m>z = f(x,y)</m> at <m>P_0(x_0, y_0, f(x_0,y_0))</m> is
<md>
<mrow>
\amp f_x(x_0,y_0)(x - x_0)
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp \quad {} + f_y(x_0,y_0)(y - y_0)
</mrow>
<mrow xml:id="eq-skel-tp-tangent-plane" number="yes">
\amp \quad {} - (z - z_0) = 0.
</mrow>
</md>
</p>
</assemblage>

<p>
The three figures below go with the derivation:
<xref ref="fig-skel-tp-direction-vectors"/> and
<xref ref="fig-skel-tp-cross-product"/> show its two steps, and
<xref ref="fig-skel-tp-plane-normal"/> shows the two tangent lines inside
the planes <m>y = y_0</m> and <m>x = x_0</m>, together with the plane
that contains both of them.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-tp-direction-vectors">
<caption>
The two tangent lines at <m>P_0</m>, each with a direction vector:
<m>\mathbf v_1</m> on the tangent line in the plane <m>y = y_0</m> and
<m>\mathbf v_2</m> on the tangent line in the plane <m>x = x_0</m>. The
dashed legs show the run and the rise of each vector.
</caption>
<image width="86%">
<shortdescription>A grey surface with two curves through a magenta point P 0, the tangent line to each curve, and an arrow along each tangent line.</shortdescription>
<description><p>
A patch of a surface around the point P 0. The green curve cut by the
plane y equals y 0 and the amber curve cut by the plane x equals x 0
cross at P 0. The teal tangent line to the green curve carries the teal
arrow v 1 from P 0, and the dark purple tangent line to the amber curve
carries the dark purple arrow v 2 from P 0. Dashed legs show each arrow
as a run parallel to an axis, the x axis for v 1 and the y axis for v 2,
followed by a vertical rise.
</p></description>
<asymptote label="asymptote-tp-direction-vectors"><![CDATA[
import graph3;
// Keep HTML interactive; use vector rendering only for print output.
if(settings.outformat != "html") settings.render=0;
size(12cm,9cm);
currentprojection=orthographic(camera=(7,5,5),up=Z);
currentlight=light(gray(0.6),specularfactor=0.2,(4,-3,6),(-4,-1,3));

// The surface, the point and the colours of fig-pd-tangent-x and
// fig-pd-tangent-y, drawn on a smaller patch around P0 so that the two
// direction vectors (each of run 1) are large enough to read.
real a=-0.6, b=1.8;
triple surfacePoint(pair p) {
  return (p.x,p.y,3-0.25*p.x^2-0.45*p.y^2);
}
surface s=surface(surfacePoint,(a,a),(b,b),12,12);
draw(s,surfacepen=gray(0.75)+opacity(0.35),
     meshpen=gray(0.55)+opacity(0.4)+linewidth(0.3pt));

real fx=-0.25, fy=-0.45;
triple P=(0.5,0.5,2.825);
triple v1=(1,0,fx), v2=(0,1,fy);
pen xTrace=rgb("008000")+linewidth(1.4pt);
pen yTrace=orange+linewidth(1.4pt);
pen xTan=rgb("009999");
pen yTan=rgb("660066");
draw(graph(new triple(real t) {return (t,0.5,2.8875-0.25*t^2);},a,b),xTrace);
draw(graph(new triple(real t) {return (0.5,t,2.9375-0.45*t^2);},a,b),yTrace);
// the two tangent lines (thin), and a direction vector on each (thick)
draw((P-1.1v1)--(P+1.15v1),xTan+linewidth(0.9pt));
draw((P-1.1v2)--(P+1.15v2),yTan+linewidth(0.9pt));
draw(P--P+v1,xTan+linewidth(3pt),Arrow3(size=12pt));
draw(P--P+v2,yTan+linewidth(3pt),Arrow3(size=12pt));

// the run and the rise of each vector, left unnamed: naming them is the exercise
pen leg=gray(0.15)+linewidth(0.8pt)+linetype("4 3");
draw(P--P+X--P+v1,leg);
draw(P--P+Y--P+v2,leg);

dot(P,magenta+linewidth(5pt));
label("$P_0$",P+0.05Z,N,magenta);
label("$\mathbf{v}_1$",P+1.18v1,SW,xTan);
label("$\mathbf{v}_2$",P+1.18v2,SE,yTan);
label("$y=y_0$",(b,0.5,2.8875-0.25*b^2),SE,xTrace);
label("$x=x_0$",(0.5,b,2.9375-0.45*b^2),E,yTrace);
label("$z=f(x,y)$",surfacePoint((1.8,-0.6)),W,gray(0.4));

pen ax=gray(0.25)+linewidth(0.6pt);
triple O=(a-0.2,a-0.2,0.7);
draw(Label("$x$",1),O--(b+0.3,a-0.2,0.7),ax,Arrow3);
draw(Label("$y$",1),O--(a-0.2,b+0.3,0.7),ax,Arrow3);
draw(Label("$z$",1),O--(a-0.2,a-0.2,3.6),ax,Arrow3);
]]></asymptote>
</image>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-tp-cross-product">
<caption>
The tangent plane at <m>P_0</m> contains <m>\mathbf v_1</m> and
<m>\mathbf v_2</m>, and with them the parallelogram they span. The cross
product <m>\mathbf v_1 \times \mathbf v_2</m> is perpendicular to both
vectors, and <m>\mathbf v_2 \times \mathbf v_1</m> points the opposite
way.
</caption>
<image width="86%">
<shortdescription>The surface with its pink tangent plane at P 0, the arrows v 1 and v 2 in the plane, and two opposite arrows perpendicular to the plane.</shortdescription>
<description><p>
The same patch of the surface and the same point P 0 as in the previous
figure, now with the pink tangent plane touching the surface at P 0. The
teal arrow v 1 and the dark purple arrow v 2 start at P 0 and lie in the
plane, and the parallelogram they span is shaded darker. A black arrow,
the cross product v 1 cross v 2, points up from P 0 at a right angle to
the plane, with small right-angle marks against v 1 and v 2. A grey
arrow, v 2 cross v 1, points from P 0 in the opposite direction, below
the plane.
</p></description>
<asymptote label="asymptote-tp-cross-product"><![CDATA[
import graph3;
// Keep HTML interactive; use vector rendering only for print output.
if(settings.outformat != "html") settings.render=0;
size(12cm,9cm);
currentprojection=orthographic(camera=(7,5,5),up=Z);
currentlight=light(gray(0.6),specularfactor=0.2,(4,-3,6),(-4,-1,3));

// the same patch of the surface as in the figure of the direction vectors
real a=-0.6, b=1.8;
triple surfacePoint(pair p) {
  return (p.x,p.y,3-0.25*p.x^2-0.45*p.y^2);
}
surface s=surface(surfacePoint,(a,a),(b,b),12,12);
draw(s,surfacepen=gray(0.75)+opacity(0.35),
     meshpen=gray(0.55)+opacity(0.4)+linewidth(0.3pt));

real fx=-0.25, fy=-0.45;
triple P=(0.5,0.5,2.825);
triple v1=(1,0,fx), v2=(0,1,fy);
triple n=cross(v1,v2);            // (-fx,-fy,1)

// the tangent plane: the points P0 + s v1 + t v2
pen planePen=rgb("e88a8a")+opacity(0.4);
pen planeEdge=rgb("993333")+linewidth(0.5pt);
path3 plane=(P-1.1v1-1.1v2)--(P+1.4v1-1.1v2)--(P+1.4v1+1.4v2)--(P-1.1v1+1.4v2)--cycle;
draw(surface(plane),planePen,nolight);
draw(plane,planeEdge);
// the parallelogram spanned by v1 and v2 lies in the tangent plane
path3 par=P--(P+v1)--(P+v1+v2)--(P+v2)--cycle;
draw(surface(par),rgb("cc4444")+opacity(0.35),nolight);
draw((P+v1)--(P+v1+v2)--(P+v2),rgb("993333")+linewidth(0.7pt)+linetype("4 3"));

pen xTan=rgb("009999");
pen yTan=rgb("660066");
draw(P--P+v1,xTan+linewidth(3pt),Arrow3(size=12pt));
draw(P--P+v2,yTan+linewidth(3pt),Arrow3(size=12pt));
draw(P--P+n,black+linewidth(3pt),Arrow3(size=12pt));
draw(P--P-n,gray(0.35)+linewidth(2.2pt),Arrow3(size=10pt));

// right-angle marks between the cross product and each of v1, v2
real m=0.16;
triple nh=unit(n), e1=unit(v1), e2=unit(v2);
draw((P+m*e1)--(P+m*e1+m*nh)--(P+m*nh),black+linewidth(0.6pt));
draw((P+m*e2)--(P+m*e2+m*nh)--(P+m*nh),black+linewidth(0.6pt));

dot(P,magenta+linewidth(5pt));
label("$P_0$",P-0.1Z,S,magenta);
label("$\mathbf{v}_1$",P+v1,NW,xTan);
label("$\mathbf{v}_2$",P+v2,E,yTan);
label("$\mathbf{v}_1\times\mathbf{v}_2$",P+n,N,black);
label("$\mathbf{v}_2\times\mathbf{v}_1$",P-n,3S,gray(0.25));
label("tangent plane",P+1.4v1-1.1v2,W,rgb("993333"));
label("$z=f(x,y)$",surfacePoint((1.8,-0.6)),W,gray(0.4));

pen ax=gray(0.25)+linewidth(0.6pt);
triple O=(a-0.2,a-0.2,0.7);
draw(Label("$x$",1),O--(b+0.3,a-0.2,0.7),ax,Arrow3);
draw(Label("$y$",1),O--(a-0.2,b+0.3,0.7),ax,Arrow3);
draw(Label("$z$",1),O--(a-0.2,a-0.2,3.6),ax,Arrow3);
]]></asymptote>
</image>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-tp-plane-normal">
<caption>
The two vertical planes <m>y = y_0</m> and <m>x = x_0</m> cut the surface
<m>z = f(x,y)</m> along the green and amber curves of
<xref ref="fig-skel-pd-tangent-x"/> and <xref ref="fig-skel-pd-tangent-y"/>, whose
tangent lines at <m>P_0</m> have slopes <m>f_x(x_0,y_0)</m> and
<m>f_y(x_0,y_0)</m>.
The tangent plane at <m>P_0</m> contains both tangent lines.
</caption>
<image width="85%">
<description>
<p>
A grey dome-shaped surface, the graph of z equals f of x y, with the pink
tangent plane touching it at the magenta point P 0. The green curve cut
by the plane y equals y 0 and the amber curve cut by the plane x equals
x 0 run across the surface through P 0, drawn in the same colors as the
two partial derivative figures. The blue tangent line to the green curve
at P 0 and the dark red tangent line to the amber curve at P 0 lie inside
the tangent plane, and a black arrow, the normal vector n, points away
from the plane at P 0, perpendicular to both tangent lines. A legend above
the picture names each of these seven elements.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\fontfamily{phv}\selectfont]
\begin{axis}[
  width=12cm, height=9.5cm,
  view={-35}{20},
  xmin=-2.3, xmax=2.3, ymin=-2.3, ymax=2.3, zmin=0, zmax=5.2,
  xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, zlabel={$z$},
  ticks=none,
  clip=false,
  % the legend of fig-cr-saddle: above the axes, two columns, Helvetica
  legend cell align=left,
  legend columns=2,
  legend style={
    at={(0.5,1.03)}, anchor=south,
    draw=gray!50, fill=white, fill opacity=0.92, text opacity=1,
    inner sep=5pt, row sep=2pt,
    /tikz/every even column/.append style={column sep=10pt},
    font=\fontfamily{phv}\fontsize{10}{12.5}\selectfont,
  },
]
% the surface z = f(x,y); same surface, point and styling as the
% partial-derivative figures fig-pd-tangent-x and fig-pd-tangent-y
\addplot3[surf, shader=flat, draw=gray!55, fill=gray!30,
          fill opacity=0.35, draw opacity=0.4,
          domain=-2:2, y domain=-2:2, samples=15, forget plot]
  {3 - 0.25*x^2 - 0.45*y^2};
% the tangent plane at P0 (pink)
\addplot3[surf, shader=flat, samples=2, domain=-1:2, y domain=-1:2,
          fill=red!30, fill opacity=0.45, draw=red!50!black, draw opacity=0.6,
          forget plot]
  {2.825 - 0.25*(x-0.5) - 0.45*(y-0.5)};
% intersection curve with y = y0 (green, as in fig-pd-tangent-x);
% samples y=0 keeps this a single curve -- without it pgfplots closes the
% parabola with a straight chord
\addplot3[very thick, green!50!black, domain=-2:2, samples=33, samples y=0,
          forget plot]
  ({x},{0.5},{2.8875 - 0.25*x^2});
% intersection curve with x = x0 (amber, as in fig-pd-tangent-y)
\addplot3[very thick, orange, domain=-2:2, samples=33, samples y=0,
          forget plot]
  ({0.5},{x},{2.9375 - 0.45*x^2});
% tangent line at P0 in the plane y = y0 (blue, as in fig-pd-tangent-x)
\addplot3[very thick, cyan!60!black, domain=-1.4:1.4, samples=2, samples y=0,
          forget plot]
  ({0.5+x},{0.5},{2.825 - 0.25*x});
% tangent line at P0 in the plane x = x0 (dark red, as in fig-pd-tangent-y)
\addplot3[very thick, purple!60!black, domain=-1.4:1.4, samples=2, samples y=0,
          forget plot]
  ({0.5},{0.5+x},{2.825 - 0.45*x});
% the normal vector n (black)
\draw[-stealth, black, very thick]
  (axis cs:0.5,0.5,2.825) -- (axis cs:0.75,0.95,3.83);
% the point P0 (magenta, as P in the partial-derivative figures)
\addplot3[only marks, mark=*, mark size=1.9, color=magenta, forget plot]
  coordinates {(0.5,0.5,2.825)};
\node[magenta, anchor=north, font=\fontfamily{phv}\selectfont]
  at (axis cs:0.5,0.3,2.66) {$P_0$};
% the legend, built by hand so that the surface gets an entry too
\addlegendimage{area legend, draw=gray!55, fill=gray!30, fill opacity=0.35}
\addlegendentry{$z=f(x,y)$}
\addlegendimage{area legend, fill=red!30, fill opacity=0.45,
                draw=red!50!black}
\addlegendentry{tangent plane at $P_0$}
\addlegendimage{very thick, green!50!black}
\addlegendentry{$z=f(x,y_0)$, cut by $y=y_0$}
\addlegendimage{very thick, orange}
\addlegendentry{$z=f(x_0,y)$, cut by $x=x_0$}
\addlegendimage{very thick, cyan!60!black}
\addlegendentry{tangent line in $y=y_0$}
\addlegendimage{very thick, purple!60!black}
\addlegendentry{tangent line in $x=x_0$}
\addlegendimage{-stealth, black, very thick}
\addlegendentry{$\mathbf n$: normal at $P_0$}
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
A normal vector to the tangent plane can also be written as a gradient.
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,10);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3.25) -- (15,3.25);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,9.75) {workspace};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,2.95) {the normal vector:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace, with a last row for the normal vector to the tangent plane.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<assemblage xml:id="skel-summary-tp-normal-vector">
<title>The Normal Vector</title>
<p>
The <term>normal vector to the tangent plane</term> of
<m>z = f(x,y)</m> at <m>P_0</m> is
<md>
<mrow>
\amp \mathbf n = \nabla w \Big|_{P_0}
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp \quad = f_x(x_0,y_0)\,\hat{\imath} + f_y(x_0,y_0)\,\hat{\jmath}
</mrow>
<mrow xml:id="eq-skel-tp-normal-vector" number="yes">
\amp \qquad {} - \hat{k},
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp w(x,y,z) = f(x,y) - z.
</mrow>
</md>
Equivalently,
<m>\mathbf n = \mathbf v_2 \times \mathbf v_1 = -(\mathbf v_1 \times \mathbf v_2)</m>,
where <m>\mathbf v_1 = \hat{\imath} + f_x(x_0,y_0)\,\hat{k}</m> and
<m>\mathbf v_2 = \hat{\jmath} + f_y(x_0,y_0)\,\hat{k}</m> are the
direction vectors of the two tangent lines.
</p>
</assemblage>

<p component="web">
<xref ref="fig-skel-tp-plane-interactive"/> rebuilds the picture above one
layer at a time, in the order of the derivation: the two slices, each
with its tangent line and its direction vector, then the cross product
of the two direction vectors, and finally the tangent plane that this
normal vector determines.
</p>
<p component="print">
An interactive figure that rebuilds the picture above one layer at a time
<mdash/> the two slices with their tangent lines and direction vectors,
then the cross product of the two vectors, then the tangent plane
<mdash/> accompanies the online version of these notes.
</p>

<sidebyside component="web" width="100%">
<figure xml:id="fig-skel-tp-plane-interactive">
<caption>
The construction of <xref ref="fig-skel-tp-plane-normal"/>, one layer at a
time. The plane <m>y = y_0</m> cuts the surface along the green curve of
<xref ref="fig-skel-pd-tangent-x"/>, whose tangent line at <m>P_0</m> has
slope <m>f_x(x_0,y_0)</m>; the plane <m>x = x_0</m> cuts it along the
amber curve of <xref ref="fig-skel-pd-tangent-y"/>, whose tangent line has
slope <m>f_y(x_0,y_0)</m>. The cross product of their direction vectors
<m>\mathbf v_1</m> and <m>\mathbf v_2</m> is perpendicular to both, and
the tangent plane is the plane through <m>P_0</m> with this normal
vector. Swapping the order of the cross product gives
<m>\mathbf n = \mathbf v_2 \times \mathbf v_1</m>.
</caption>
<!-- aspect 1:1 rather than 3:2: like limit-paths-manim.html, the equation
     card and the readout sit in a bar above the picture (and the caption in
     a strip below it) rather than overlaying it, so the frame needs the
     extra height to keep the surface itself large enough for the direction
     vectors and their labels to be read. -->
<interactive xml:id="interactive-skel-tp-plane"
             iframe="tangent-plane-manim.html" width="100%" aspect="1:1">
<instructions>
<p>
The surface and the point <m>P_0</m> are those of the figure above. Add
the layers with the buttons <mdash/> the slice <m>y = y_0</m> with its
blue tangent line and the direction vector <m>\mathbf v_1</m>, the slice
<m>x = x_0</m> with its purple tangent line and the direction vector
<m>\mathbf v_2</m>, the cross product
<m>\mathbf v_1 \times \mathbf v_2</m> with the parallelogram the two
vectors span, and the pink tangent plane <mdash/> in any order, or press
<q>Build it up</q> to watch them arrive one at a time. <q>Swap</q>
turns the cross product into
<m>\mathbf v_2 \times \mathbf v_1 = \mathbf n</m>, which points the
opposite way. The vectors <m>\mathbf v_1</m>, <m>\mathbf v_2</m> and
their cross product are collected in the panel above the picture. <q>Side view</q> looks
straight down the <m>y</m> axis so the slope of the blue tangent line can
be read directly, <q>Spin</q> turns the picture, dragging rotates it
by hand, and the button at the bottom right shows the figure full screen.
</p>
</instructions>
</interactive>
</figure>
</sidebyside>

<p component="web">
Everything in <xref ref="fig-skel-tp-plane-normal"/> can also be explored
interactively in the <dataurl source="tangent-plane-explorer.html">Tangent
Plane Explorer</dataurl>, which opens in a new tab. It builds the picture
up one layer at a time <mdash/> the surface, the point <m>P_0</m>, the two
cutting planes and the curves they cut, the two tangent lines, the tangent
plane, and finally <m>\mathbf n</m> <mdash/> for any of several surfaces.
Drag <m>P_0</m> around and rotate the figure to see for yourself that the
tangent plane always contains both tangent lines and that <m>\mathbf n</m>
always stands at a right angle to it.
</p>

<p>
Finally, write the parametric equations of the line normal to the
surface at <m>P_0</m>.
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,6);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,5.7) {the normal line at $P_0$:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box for the parametric equations of the normal line to the surface at P nought.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<assemblage xml:id="skel-summary-tp-normal-line">
<title>The Normal Line</title>
<p>
The parametric equations of the <term>normal line to the surface</term>
<m>z = f(x,y)</m> at <m>P_0(x_0, y_0, f(x_0,y_0))</m> are
<md>
<mrow>
x(t) \amp= x_0 + f_x(x_0,y_0)\,t,
</mrow>
<mrow xml:id="eq-skel-tp-normal-line" number="yes">
y(t) \amp= y_0 + f_y(x_0,y_0)\,t,
</mrow>
<mrow>
z(t) \amp= f(x_0,y_0) - t, \qquad t \in \R.
</mrow>
</md>
</p>
</assemblage>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="subsec-skel-tp-example-paraboloid">
<title>Tangent Plane and Normal Line to a Surface</title>

<p>
In the first example, we find the normal line and the tangent plane to a
paraboloid at a given point.
</p>

<example xml:id="ex-skel-tp-paraboloid">
<title>Tangent Plane and Normal Line to a Paraboloid</title>
<statement>
<p>
Consider the surface <m>z = f(x,y) = -x^2 - y^2</m> and the point
<m>P_0(1, 1, -2)</m>.
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
Find the equation of the normal line to the surface at <m>P_0</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Find the equation of the tangent plane to the surface at <m>P_0</m>.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>

<p>
<em>A.</em>
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,10);
\draw[gray!35] (0,4.5) -- (15,4.5);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,9.75) {workspace};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,4.2) {the normal line:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace, with a last row for the parametric equations of the normal line.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<p>
<em>B.</em>
(Compare both answers with <xref ref="fig-skel-tp-example-paraboloid"/>.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,7);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,6.7) {the tangent plane:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box for the equation of the tangent plane to the paraboloid at P nought.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-tp-example-paraboloid">
<caption>
The graph of <m>z = -x^2 - y^2</m>, its tangent plane (green), and its
normal line (black) at the point <m>P_0(1,1,-2)</m>. Compare with what
you found.
</caption>
<image width="80%">
<shortdescription>The paraboloid z equals negative x squared minus y squared with its tangent plane and normal line at the point 1, 1, negative 2.</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
A downward-opening paraboloid drawn as a blue mesh, the graph of z equals
negative x squared minus y squared. A green tangent plane patch touches
the surface at the red point P 0 with coordinates 1, 1, negative 2, on
the side of the dome. A black line passes through P 0 perpendicular to
the plane: the normal line to the surface. Labels beside them name the
surface, the point P 0, the tangent plane and the normal line.
</p>
</description>
<asymptote label="asymptote-tp-example-paraboloid"><![CDATA[
import graph3;
// Keep HTML interactive; use vector rendering only for print output.
if(settings.outformat != "html") settings.render=0;
size(11.5cm,11cm);
currentprojection=orthographic(camera=(9,-1,5),up=Z);
currentlight=light(gray(0.6),specularfactor=0.2,(4,-3,6),(-4,-1,3));

// the surface z = -x^2 - y^2 over the disc of radius 2.4
triple dome(pair p) {return (p.x*cos(p.y),p.x*sin(p.y),-p.x^2);}
surface s=surface(dome,(0,0),(2.4,2pi),12,36);
draw(s,surfacepen=rgb(0.82,0.86,1)+opacity(0.55),
     meshpen=rgb(0.25,0.35,0.85)+opacity(0.55)+linewidth(0.3pt));

// the tangent plane -2x - 2y - z = -2 at P0(1,1,-2)
triple P0=(1,1,-2);
// a square patch of it, centred at P0: e1 is horizontal and e2 runs down the plane
triple e1=unit((1,-1,0)), e2=unit((1,1,-4));
pen planeFill=rgb("5fae5f")+opacity(0.5);
pen planeEdge=rgb("1f5f1f")+linewidth(0.6pt);
real h=1.05;
path3 patch=(P0-h*e1-h*e2)--(P0+h*e1-h*e2)--(P0+h*e1+h*e2)--(P0-h*e1+h*e2)--cycle;
draw(surface(patch),planeFill,nolight);
draw(patch,planeEdge);

// the normal line (1 - 2t, 1 - 2t, -2 - t)
triple n=(-2,-2,-1);
draw((P0-0.6n)--(P0+0.45n),black+linewidth(1.6pt));

dot(P0,red+linewidth(6pt));

pen ax=gray(0.25)+linewidth(0.6pt);
draw(Label("$x$",1),(-2.3,0,0)--(3.1,0,0),ax,Arrow3);
draw(Label("$y$",1),(0,-2.3,0)--(0,3.1,0),ax,Arrow3);
draw(Label("$z$",1),(0,0,-6.6)--(0,0,2.2),ax,Arrow3);
// a few ticks, to place the point (1,1,-2)
pen tick=gray(0.25)+linewidth(0.6pt);
for(int k=-6; k <= 2; k += 2) {
  if(k != 0) {
    draw((0,-0.08,k)--(0,0.08,k),tick);
    label("$"+string(k)+"$",(0,-0.08,k),W,fontsize(8pt)+gray(0.25));
  }
}
draw((2,-0.08,0)--(2,0.08,0),tick);
label("$2$",(2,-0.08,0),W,fontsize(8pt)+gray(0.25));
draw((-0.08,2,0)--(0.08,2,0),tick);
label("$2$",(0.08,2,0),S,fontsize(8pt)+gray(0.25));

// named beside the patch and in front of it, so that the plane does not hide the name
label("$P_0(1,1,-2)$",P0+(h+0.1)*e1-0.2*unit(n),W,red);
label("tangent plane",P0-h*e1-h*e2,NE,rgb("1f5f1f"));
label("normal line",P0-0.6n,E,black);
label("$z=-x^2-y^2$",dome((2.4,pi/2)),E,rgb(0.2,0.3,0.75));
]]></asymptote>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
<xref ref="fig-skel-tp-normal-vector-video"/> works through this
computation from the start: it builds the gradient <m>\nabla w</m>,
evaluates it at <m>P_0</m> to get the normal vector, and then draws the
normal line and the tangent plane on the surface. Note that the video
takes the normal vector in the outward direction,
<m>\mathbf n = -\nabla w \big|_{P_0}</m>, the opposite of the
<m>\nabla w \big|_{P_0}</m> used above; either
choice gives the same normal line and the same tangent plane, since the
two vectors differ only by the factor <m>-1</m>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-tp-normal-vector-video">
<caption>
Computing the normal vector for <xref ref="ex-skel-tp-paraboloid"/>. The
surface, the point <m>P_0(1,1,-2)</m>, the gradient computation, the
normal vector, the normal line, and the tangent plane are built up in
turn, followed by a slow rotation of the finished figure.
</caption>
<video xml:id="video-skel-tp-computing-normal-vector"
       source="videos/ComputingNormalVector.mp4"
       preview="videos/previews/ComputingNormalVector.jpg"
       width="90%"/>
</figure>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="subsec-skel-tp-change">
<title>Estimating the Change in a Particular Direction</title>

<p>
Before turning to functions of two variables, it is worth recalling the
analogous idea from single-variable calculus. For a differentiable
function <m>y = f(x)</m>, moving a small distance <m>dx</m> away from a
point <m>x</m> changes the height of the curve by an amount that can be
estimated from the slope <m>f'(x)</m>: the resulting
<term>differential</term> is
<md xml:id="eq-skel-tp-1d-df" number="yes">
df = f'(x)\,dx.
</md>
As <xref ref="fig-skel-tp-linear-approx-1d"/> shows, this differential
closely approximates the actual change in the function,
<m>\Delta f = f(x+dx) - f(x)</m>, as long as <m>dx</m> is small.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-tp-linear-approx-1d">
<caption>
The tangent line at <m>x</m> approximates <m>y = f(x)</m> near
<m>x + dx</m>. The differential <m>df = f'(x)\,dx</m>, found by walking
along the tangent line, closely approximates the actual change
<m>\Delta f = f(x+dx) - f(x)</m>, found by walking along the curve.
</caption>
<image width="72%">
<shortdescription>The graph of y equals f of x with its tangent line at x, comparing the differential d f with the actual change delta f over a step d x.</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
The graph of a curve y equals f of x. At a point x on the curve, a
right triangle formed by the tangent line has horizontal leg d x and
vertical leg d f, equal to f prime of x times d x. A vertical bracket to
the left, labeled delta f, marks the actual rise of the curve, f of x
plus d x minus f of x, and brackets to the right show the differential
d f equals f prime of x times d x above the baseline height f of x. The
curve ends slightly above the tangent line at x plus d x.
</p>
</description>
<asymptote label="asymptote-tp-linear-approx-1d"><![CDATA[
import graph;
size(11cm,7.6cm,IgnoreAspect);

// y = f(x), the point x and the step dx
real f(real t) {return exp(0.35*t);}
real x0=2.5, dx=1;
real y0=f(x0), y1=f(x0+dx);
real yt=y0+0.35*y0*dx;                 // the height reached along the tangent line

pen curvePen=rgb("D55E00")+linewidth(1.4pt);
pen tanPen=rgb("0072B2")+linewidth(1.6pt);
pen guide=gray(0.5)+linewidth(0.6pt)+linetype("4 3");
pen dim=black+linewidth(0.6pt);

draw((-0.3,0)--(6.1,0),black+linewidth(0.9pt),Arrow(6));
draw((0,-0.3)--(0,4.8),black+linewidth(0.9pt),Arrow(6));
label("$x$",(6.1,0),E);
label("$y$",(0,4.8),N);

draw((x0,0)--(x0,y0)--(0,y0),guide);
draw((x0+dx,0)--(x0+dx,y1)--(0,y1),guide);
draw((x0+dx,y0)--(5.5,y0),guide);
draw((x0+dx,yt)--(5.5,yt),guide);

draw(graph(f,0,4.25),curvePen,Arrow(6));
label("$y=f(x)$",(4.25,f(4.25)),E,curvePen);

// the tangent line at x, and the triangle with legs dx and df
draw((x0-0.9,y0-0.9*0.35*y0)--(x0+1.5*dx,y0+1.5*0.35*y0*dx),tanPen+linewidth(0.7pt));
draw((x0,y0)--(x0+dx,yt)--(x0+dx,y0)--cycle,tanPen);

// dx, df, f(x) and the actual change Delta f
draw((x0,y0-0.42)--(x0+dx,y0-0.42),dim,Arrows(4));
label("$dx$",(x0+dx/2,y0-0.42),S);
draw((4.9,y0)--(4.9,yt),dim,Arrows(4));
label("$df=f'(x)\,dx$",(4.9,(y0+yt)/2),E,tanPen);
draw((4.9,0)--(4.9,y0),dim,Arrows(4));
label("$f(x)$",(4.9,y0/2),E);
draw((1.3,y0)--(1.3,y1),dim,Arrows(4));
label("$\Delta f$",(1.3,(y0+y1)/2),W,curvePen);

dot((x0,y0),curvePen+linewidth(4.5pt));
dot((x0+dx,y1),curvePen+linewidth(4.5pt));
dot((x0+dx,yt),tanPen+linewidth(4.5pt));

label("$x$",(x0,0),S);
label("$x+dx$",(x0+dx,0),S);
label("$f(x)$",(0,y0),W);
label("$f(x+dx)$",(0,y1),W);
]]></asymptote>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
The same question arises for a function of two variables. The
difference is that near a point in the plane there is no longer a
single direction to move in <mdash/> moving away from a point requires
both a distance and a direction. Consider the function <m>z = f(x,y)</m>,
a point <m>P_0(x_0,y_0)</m> in its domain, and a unit vector
<m>\hat{u} = u_1\,\hat{\imath} + u_2\,\hat{\jmath}</m>. The question is:
</p>

<blockquote>
<p>
<em>How can we estimate the change in <m>f</m> (<m>\Delta f</m>) as a
result of moving a small distance (<m>ds</m>) away from <m>P_0</m> in the
direction <m>\hat{u}</m>?</em>
</p>
</blockquote>

<p>
Name the point <m>P</m> that this move reaches, and write down the
change it causes in the value of <m>f</m>.
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,8);
\draw[gray!35] (0,4) -- (15,4);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,7.7) {the point $P$ it reaches:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,3.7) {$\Delta f =$};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in two rows, one for the point reached and one for the resulting change in the value of the function.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<assemblage xml:id="skel-summary-tp-delta-f">
<title>The Exact Change</title>
<p>
Moving away from <m>P_0(x_0,y_0)</m> by the distance <m>ds</m> in the
direction <m>\hat{u}</m> takes us to
<m>P(x_0 + u_1\,ds,\; y_0 + u_2\,ds)</m> and causes the change
<md>
<mrow>
\Delta f \amp = f(x_0 + u_1\,ds,\; y_0 + u_2\,ds)
</mrow>
<mrow xml:id="eq-skel-tp-delta-f" number="yes">
\amp \quad {} - f(x_0, y_0)
</mrow>
</md>
in the value of the function.
</p>
</assemblage>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-tp-change-schematic">
<caption>
Moving away from <m>P_0(x_0,y_0)</m> by the distance <m>ds</m> in the
direction <m>\hat{u} = \langle u_1, u_2 \rangle</m> is the displacement
<m>\hat{u}\,ds = u_1\,ds\,\hat{\imath} + u_2\,ds\,\hat{\jmath}</m> in the
<m>xy</m>-plane. It takes us to the point <m>P</m> and causes the change
<m>\Delta f = f(x_0 + u_1\,ds,\; y_0 + u_2\,ds) - f(x_0,y_0)</m> in the
value of the function, the difference between the heights of the surface
<m>z = f(x,y)</m> above <m>P</m> and above <m>P_0</m>.
</caption>
<image width="90%">
<shortdescription>A surface above the x y plane, with the displacement from P 0 to P in the plane and the change delta f in the height of the surface.</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
In the x y plane, a black arrow u d s leaves the blue point P 0 with
coordinates x 0, y 0 and ends at the red point P with coordinates x 0
plus u 1 d s, y 0 plus u 2 d s. Its component u 1 d s i along the x axis
is drawn in orange and its component u 2 d s j along the y axis in green.
Above the plane is a grey surface, the graph of z equals f of x y. Dashed
vertical segments rise from P 0 and from P to the surface, where a blue
point marks the height f of x 0, y 0 and a red point marks the height of
f at P; a black curve on the surface joins them. A dashed horizontal
segment carries the first height across to the second vertical segment,
and a red double arrow between the two heights is labeled delta f.
</p>
</description>
<asymptote label="asymptote-tp-change"><![CDATA[
import graph3;
// Keep HTML interactive; use vector rendering only for print output.
if(settings.outformat != "html") settings.render=0;
size(12cm,9.5cm);
currentprojection=orthographic(camera=(6,-7,4.5),up=Z);
currentlight=light(gray(0.6),specularfactor=0.2,(4,-3,6),(-4,-1,3));

// a general surface z = f(x,y); its formula is only a prop
real f(real x, real y) {return 1.3+0.28*x^2+0.22*y^2;}
triple surfacePoint(pair p) {return (p.x,p.y,f(p.x,p.y));}
surface s=surface(surfacePoint,(0.15,0.1),(2.35,2.1),12,12);
draw(s,surfacepen=gray(0.75)+opacity(0.3),
     meshpen=gray(0.55)+opacity(0.35)+linewidth(0.3pt));

// P0, the displacement u ds and the point P, all in the xy-plane
pair p0=(0.8,0.7), d=(0.9,0.75);
pair p1=p0+d;
triple P0=(p0.x,p0.y,0), P=(p1.x,p1.y,0);
triple S0=surfacePoint(p0), S1=surfacePoint(p1);
pen c0=rgb("0072B2"), c1=rgb("D55E00");
pen cx=rgb("E69F00"), cy=rgb("009E73");

draw(P0--(p1.x,p0.y,0),cx+linewidth(2pt),Arrow3(size=8pt));
draw((p1.x,p0.y,0)--P,cy+linewidth(2pt),Arrow3(size=8pt));
draw(P0--P,black+linewidth(2.4pt),Arrow3(size=10pt));

// the two values of f, and the curve on the surface above the path
pen riser=gray(0.4)+linewidth(0.7pt)+linetype("4 3");
draw(P0--S0,riser);
draw(P--S1,riser);
draw(graph(new triple(real t) {return surfacePoint(p0+t*d);},0,1),black+linewidth(1.2pt));
triple Q=(p1.x,p1.y,S0.z);
draw(S0--Q,riser);
draw(Q--S1,c1+linewidth(1.6pt),Arrows3(size=6pt));

dot(P0,c0+linewidth(5pt));
dot(P,c1+linewidth(5pt));
dot(S0,c0+linewidth(5pt));
dot(S1,c1+linewidth(5pt));

label("$P_0(x_0,y_0)$",P0,W,c0);
label("$P(x_0+u_1\,ds,\;y_0+u_2\,ds)$",P,NE,c1);
label("$\hat{u}\,ds$",P0+0.5(P-P0),N,black);
label("$u_1\,ds\,\hat{\imath}$",(p0.x+0.5*d.x,p0.y,0),SW,cx);
label("$u_2\,ds\,\hat{\jmath}$",(p1.x,p0.y+0.4*d.y,0),SE,cy);
label("$f(x_0,y_0)$",S0,W,c0);
label("$f(x_0+u_1\,ds,\;y_0+u_2\,ds)$",S1,NE,c1);
label("$\Delta f$",0.5(Q+S1),E,c1);
label("$z=f(x,y)$",surfacePoint((0.15,2.1)),N,gray(0.4));

pen ax=gray(0.25)+linewidth(0.6pt);
draw(Label("$x$",1),O--(2.9,0,0),ax,Arrow3);
draw(Label("$y$",1),O--(0,2.7,0),ax,Arrow3);
draw(Label("$z$",1),O--(0,0,3.6),ax,Arrow3);
]]></asymptote>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
Computing <xref ref="eq-skel-tp-delta-f"/> exactly means evaluating
<m>f</m> twice. We now derive an estimate of <m>\Delta f</m> instead.
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,10);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3.25) -- (15,3.25);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,9.75) {workspace};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,2.95) {the estimate:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace, with a last row for the estimate of the change.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<assemblage xml:id="skel-summary-tp-df">
<title>The Differential</title>
<p>
The rate of change of <m>f</m> at <m>P_0</m> in the direction
<m>\hat{u}</m> is
<md xml:id="eq-skel-tp-dirderiv" number="yes">
\left(\frac{df}{ds}\right)_{\hat{u}, P_0}
= \nabla f \Big|_{P_0} \cdot \hat{u},
</md>
so the change in <m>f</m> over a distance <m>ds</m> is estimated by
<md xml:id="eq-skel-tp-df" number="yes">
\Delta f \approx df
= \left(\nabla f \Big|_{P_0} \cdot \hat{u}\right) ds.
</md>
The quantity <m>df</m> is called the <term>differential</term> of
<m>f</m>.
</p>
</assemblage>

<example xml:id="ex-skel-tp-change">
<title>Estimating the Change in a Given Direction</title>
<statement>
<p>
Consider the function <m>f(x,y) = 1 + x^2 + y^2</m>, the point
<m>P_0(1,1)</m>, and the unit vector
<m>\hat{u} = \frac{1}{\sqrt 2}\,\hat{\imath} +
\frac{1}{\sqrt 2}\,\hat{\jmath}</m>. Estimate the change in the value of
<m>f</m> as a result of moving away from <m>P_0(1,1)</m> in the
direction of <m>\hat{u}</m> by <m>0.1</m> units.
</p>

<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
(Compare with <xref ref="fig-skel-tp-example-change-zoom"/>.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,12);
\draw[gray!35] (0,4) -- (15,4);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,11.75) {workspace};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,3.7) {the estimated change:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace, with a last row for the estimated change in f.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-tp-example-change">
<caption>
The graph of <m>z = 1 + x^2 + y^2</m> and the vertical
plane <m>x - y = 0</m> that contains the direction <m>\hat{u}</m>.
Moving from <m>P_0(1,1,0)</m> by <m>ds = 0.1</m> along <m>\hat{u}</m>
moves the input point (light blue), and the value of <m>f</m> climbs
along the black intersection curve from <m>f(1,1) = 3</m> (green) to the
orange point. The blue line is the tangent line to that curve at the
green point.
</caption>
<image xml:id="image-skel-tp-change-3d" width="75%">
<shortdescription>The paraboloid z equals 1 plus x squared plus y squared cut by the vertical plane y equals x, with the points of the example and the tangent line.</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
An upward-opening gray paraboloid, the graph of z equals 1 plus x squared
plus y squared, cut by a green vertical plane through the line y equals
x. The plane meets the surface along a black parabola. On the base plane,
a black arrow shows the unit vector u leaving the point 1, 1, 0. Four
points are marked and labeled: the input point P 0 at 1, 1, 0 in dark
red, the moved input point P in light blue just beyond it, the output
point at height f of P 0 equals 3 in green on the curve, and the moved
output point at height about 3.29 in orange on the curve; a dashed gray
segment joins each input point to its output point. A blue line through
the green point is the tangent line to the parabola there.
</p>
</description>
<asymptote label="asymptote-tp-example-change"><![CDATA[
import graph3;
// Keep HTML interactive; use vector rendering only for print output.
if(settings.outformat != "html") settings.render=0;
size(11.5cm,11cm);
currentprojection=orthographic(camera=(8,-3,3.2),up=Z);
currentlight=light(gray(0.6),specularfactor=0.2,(4,-3,6),(-4,-1,3));

// the paraboloid z = 1 + x^2 + y^2 over the disc of radius 2.1
triple bowl(pair p) {return (p.x*cos(p.y),p.x*sin(p.y),1+p.x^2);}
surface s=surface(bowl,(0,0),(2.1,2pi),12,36);
draw(s,surfacepen=gray(0.75)+opacity(0.28),
     meshpen=gray(0.55)+opacity(0.3)+linewidth(0.3pt));

// the vertical plane x - y = 0, which contains the direction u
real h=sqrt(0.5);
pen planeFill=rgb("66cc66")+opacity(0.25);
pen planeEdge=rgb("2e7d32")+linewidth(0.5pt);
path3 plane=(-1.7,-1.7,0)--(1.7,1.7,0)--(1.7,1.7,6.2)--(-1.7,-1.7,6.2)--cycle;
draw(surface(plane),planeFill,nolight);
draw(plane,planeEdge);

// the curve cut by the plane, and its tangent line at (1,1,3)
draw(graph(new triple(real t) {return (t,t,1+2t^2);},-1.6,1.6),black+linewidth(1.5pt));
pen tanPen=rgb("1f4fb5")+linewidth(1.3pt);
triple A=(1,1,3), T=(h,h,2*sqrt(2));       // T: one unit of s along the tangent line
draw((A-0.55T)--(A+0.9T),tanPen);

// u at P0, and the step ds = 0.1
triple P0=(1,1,0), P=(1+0.1h,1+0.1h,0);
triple B=(P.x,P.y,1+P.x^2+P.y^2);
draw(P0--P0+(h,h,0),black+linewidth(2pt),Arrow3(size=8pt));
pen riser=gray(0.4)+linewidth(0.7pt)+linetype("4 3");
draw(P0--A,riser);
draw(P--B,riser);

pen cP0=rgb("8e1b3c"), cP=rgb("1ea7c5"), cA=rgb("2e8b2e"), cB=orange;
dot(P0,cP0+linewidth(5pt));
dot(P,cP+linewidth(5pt));
dot(A,cA+linewidth(5pt));
dot(B,cB+linewidth(5pt));

label("$\hat{u}$",P0+(h,h,0),E,black);
label("$P_0=(1,1,0)$",P0,W,cP0);
label("$P=P_0+\hat{u}\,ds$",P,2S+E,cP);
label("$f(P_0)=3$",A,2NW,cA);
label("$f(P)\approx 3.29$",B,2E+S,cB);
label("tangent line",A-0.55T,E,tanPen);
label("plane $x-y=0$",(-1.7,-1.7,6.2),NW,rgb("2e7d32"));
label("$z=1+x^2+y^2$",bowl((2.1,pi/2)),2N+E,gray(0.4));

pen ax=gray(0.25)+linewidth(0.6pt);
draw(Label("$x$",1),(-2.4,0,0)--(2.9,0,0),ax,Arrow3);
draw(Label("$y$",1),(0,-2.4,0)--(0,2.9,0),ax,Arrow3);
draw(Label("$z$",1),O--(0,0,6.6),ax,Arrow3);
]]></asymptote>
</image>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-tp-example-change-zoom">
<caption>
The curve of <xref ref="fig-skel-tp-example-change"/> plotted against the
distance <m>s</m> travelled along <m>\hat{u}</m>, together with its
tangent line at <m>P_0</m>. The window starts at <m>s = 0.05</m>, past
the stretch where the two are indistinguishable, so that the gap between
them can be seen. At <m>s = ds = 0.1</m> the blue point is the estimate
<m>f(P_0) + df</m> and the orange point the exact value; compare with
what you found.
</caption>
<image xml:id="image-skel-tp-change-zoom" width="65%">
<shortdescription>The value of f along u and its tangent line at P 0, zoomed in around s equals 0.1, where a small gap separates them.</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
A two dimensional graph zoomed into the neighborhood of the moved point.
The horizontal axis is the distance s travelled from P 0 in the direction
of u, running from 0.05 to 0.15 rather than from 0, so that the two
graphs are far enough apart to be told apart; the vertical axis is z,
running from about 3.13 to about 3.46. A black curve is the value of f
along u, and a blue dashed line is its tangent line at P 0. The line
stays just below the curve and the gap between them widens to the right.
At s equals d s equals 0.1, dashed guide lines pick out the two heights:
the tangent line reaches the blue point, the estimate 3 plus d f, and the
curve reaches the orange point, the exact value of f at the moved point.
A bracket labels the gap between the two points as delta f minus d f.
</p>
</description>
<asymptote label="asymptote-tp-example-change-zoom"><![CDATA[
import graph;
size(10.5cm,9.5cm,IgnoreAspect);

// f along u, as a function of the distance s from P0: z = 3 + 2 sqrt2 s + s^2
real g(real s) {return 3+2*sqrt(2)*s+s^2;}
real L(real s) {return 3+2*sqrt(2)*s;}      // its tangent line at s = 0
real a=0.048, b=0.153, lo=3.128, hi=3.462, ds=0.1;

pen curvePen=black+linewidth(1.5pt);
pen tanPen=rgb("1f4fb5")+linewidth(1.5pt)+linetype("6 4");
pen guide=gray(0.55)+linewidth(0.6pt)+linetype("4 3");
pen gapPen=rgb("8e1b3c");

// axes, drawn by hand so that the tick at s = ds can carry its own name
draw((a,lo)--(b,lo),black+linewidth(0.8pt),Arrow(5));
draw((a,lo)--(a,hi),black+linewidth(0.8pt),Arrow(5));
real[] xt={0.05,0.075,0.1,0.125,0.15};
string[] xl={"$0.05$","","$ds=0.1$","","$0.15$"};
for(int i=0; i < xt.length; ++i) {
  draw((xt[i],lo)--(xt[i],lo-0.004),black+linewidth(0.6pt));
  if(xl[i] != "") label(xl[i],(xt[i],lo-0.004),S);
}
for(real y=3.15; y < 3.46; y += 0.05) {
  draw((a,y)--(a-0.0012,y),black+linewidth(0.6pt));
  label("$"+format("%.2f",y)+"$",(a-0.0012,y),W);
}
label("$s$: distance from $P_0$ along $\hat{u}$",((a+b)/2,lo-0.022),S);
label("$z$",(a,hi),N);

// the two heights reached over ds = 0.1
draw((a,L(ds))--(ds,L(ds)),guide);
draw((a,g(ds))--(ds,g(ds)),guide);
draw((ds,lo)--(ds,g(ds)),guide);

draw(graph(g,a,b-0.002),curvePen);
draw(graph(L,a,b-0.002),tanPen);
label("$f$ along $\hat{u}$",(0.135,g(0.135)),NW,black);
label("tangent line at $P_0$",(0.135,L(0.135)),SE,rgb("1f4fb5"));

dot((ds,L(ds)),rgb("1f4fb5")+linewidth(5pt));
dot((ds,g(ds)),orange+linewidth(5pt));
label("exact $f(P)$",(ds,g(ds)),(-1,2.2),orange);
label("estimate $3+df$",(ds,L(ds)),(1,-2.4),rgb("1f4fb5"));

// the gap between the two at s = ds
draw((ds+0.004,L(ds))--(ds+0.004,g(ds)),gapPen+linewidth(0.8pt),Bars(4));
label("$\Delta f-df$",(ds+0.004,(L(ds)+g(ds))/2),E,gapPen);
]]></asymptote>
</image>
</figure>

<p component="web">
<xref ref="fig-skel-tp-change-interactive"/> makes the same comparison
for every direction <m>\hat{u}</m> and every distance <m>ds</m>.
</p>
<p component="print">
An interactive figure that compares the differential <m>df</m> with the
actual change <m>\Delta f</m>, for every direction <m>\hat{u}</m> and
every distance <m>ds</m>, accompanies the online version of these notes.
</p>

<sidebyside component="web" width="100%">
<figure xml:id="fig-skel-tp-change-interactive">
<caption>
The differential <m>df</m> against the actual change <m>\Delta f</m> for
<m>f(x,y) = 1 + x^2 + y^2</m> at <m>P_0(1,1)</m>. The vertical plane
through <m>P_0</m> along <m>\hat{u}</m> cuts the surface in the gold
curve. Over the distance <m>ds</m>, the tangent line to that curve (blue)
changes its height by <m>df</m>, while the surface itself changes its
height by <m>\Delta f</m> (orange); the red segment between the two is
the error <m>\Delta f - df</m>.
</caption>
<!-- aspect 5:6: the readout sits in a bar above the picture and the sliders
     and buttons in a panel below it, all in type large enough to read in the
     book's frame, so the frame is taller than it is wide to leave the
     picture its room. -->
<interactive xml:id="interactive-skel-tp-change"
             iframe="differential-estimate-manim.html" width="100%" aspect="5:6">
<instructions>
<p>
The surface and the point <m>P_0</m> are those of
<xref ref="ex-skel-tp-change"/>. Choose the direction of
<m>\hat{u}</m> and the distance <m>ds</m> with the two sliders; the panel
above the picture reports the rate of change
<m>\nabla f \Big|_{P_0} \cdot \hat{u}</m>, the estimate <m>df</m>, the
actual change <m>\Delta f</m> and their difference. <q>Example</q> sets
the direction and the distance of the example, <q>Shrink
<m>ds</m></q> lets the distance go to zero, and <q>Turn
<m>\hat{u}</m></q> takes the direction once around <m>P_0</m>.
<q>Zoom in</q> moves the camera close to <m>P_0</m>, <q>Slice view</q>
looks straight at the vertical plane so that the picture becomes the
single-variable one of <xref ref="fig-skel-tp-linear-approx-1d"/>,
<q>Tangent plane</q> adds the tangent plane at <m>P_0</m>, which contains
the tangent line of every direction, dragging rotates the picture by
hand, and the button at the bottom right shows the figure full screen.
</p>
</instructions>
</interactive>
</figure>
</sidebyside>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="subsec-skel-tp-linearization">
<title>Linearizing <m>z = f(x,y)</m></title>

<p>
Before generalizing to two variables, recall how a differentiable
function <m>y = f(x)</m> is approximated near a point <m>x_0</m> by its
tangent line. This line passes through <m>\bigl(x_0, f(x_0)\bigr)</m>
with slope <m>f'(x_0)</m>, so it is the graph of the linear function
<md xml:id="eq-skel-tp-linearization-1d" number="yes">
L(x) = f(x_0) + f'(x_0)(x - x_0).
</md>
As <xref ref="fig-skel-tp-linearization-1d"/> shows, <m>L(x)</m> matches
both the value and the slope of <m>f</m> at <m>x_0</m>, so its graph hugs
the curve nearby.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-tp-linearization-1d">
<caption>
The tangent line <m>L(x) = f(x_0) + f'(x_0)(x-x_0)</m> approximates
<m>y = f(x)</m> near <m>x_0</m>.
</caption>
<image width="72%">
<description>
<p>
The graph of a curve y equals f of x and its tangent line at the point
x0. The tangent line passes through the point P0 with coordinates x0,
f of x0, matching both the height and the slope of the curve there.
</p>
</description>
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="skel-prefigure-tp-linearization-1d">
<diagram dimensions="(500,340)" margins="14">
<definition>x0 = 2.5</definition>
<definition>fx(x) = exp(0.35*x)</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-1.3,-0.5,6.2,4.9)">
<vector at="lz-xaxis" tail="(-0.3,0)" v="(6.3,0)" stroke="black" thickness="2"/>
<vector at="lz-yaxis" tail="(0,-0.3)" v="(0,5.1)" stroke="black" thickness="2"/>
<graph at="lz-curve" function="fx" domain="(0,4.3)"
       arrows="1" stroke="#D55E00" thickness="2"/>
<tangent-line at="lz-tangent" function="fx" point="x0" infinite="yes"
              stroke="#0072B2" thickness="2"/>
<line at="lz-guidex" p1="(2.5,0)" p2="(2.5,2.398875)"
      stroke="#7F7F7F" thickness="1.3" dash="4 3"/>
<line at="lz-guidey" p1="(2.5,2.398875)" p2="(0,2.398875)"
      stroke="#7F7F7F" thickness="1.3" dash="4 3"/>
<point at="lz-P0" p="(2.5,2.398875)" fill="black" radius="3"/>
<label at="lz-xlabel" anchor="(6.0,-0.15)" alignment="n"><m>x</m></label>
<label at="lz-ylabel" anchor="(-0.05,4.8)" alignment="w"><m>y</m></label>
<label at="lz-curvelabel" anchor="(0.6,1.55)" alignment="n" color="#D55E00">
<m>y=f(x)</m>
</label>
<!-- both labels sit below the tangent line, where the picture is empty:
     the curve is convex, so it stays above the line everywhere -->
<label at="lz-tangentlabel" anchor="(4.95,4.456)" alignment="se" color="#0072B2">
<m>L(x)</m>
</label>
<label at="lz-x0-tick" anchor="(2.5,-0.1)" alignment="s"><m>x_0</m></label>
<label at="lz-fx0-tick" anchor="(-0.05,2.398875)" alignment="w"><m>f(x_0)</m></label>
<label at="lz-P0-label" anchor="(2.66,2.33)" alignment="se"><m>P_0</m></label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="The graph of a function y equals f of x and its tangent line at the point x0.">
  <annotation ref="lz-curve" text="The graph of the function y equals f of x."/>
  <annotation ref="lz-tangent" text="The tangent line to the graph at the point P0, given by L of x equals f of x0 plus f prime of x0 times x minus x0."/>
  <annotation ref="lz-P0" text="The point P0 with coordinates x0, f of x0, where the tangent line touches the curve."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
The same idea extends to a function of two variables <m>z = f(x,y)</m>.
Near a point <m>P_0(x_0,y_0,f(x_0,y_0))</m> on the surface, the
<term>tangent plane</term> plays the role that the tangent line played
above, and the single slope <m>f'(x_0)</m> is replaced by the two
partial derivatives <m>f_x(x_0,y_0)</m> and <m>f_y(x_0,y_0)</m> <mdash/>
one for the rate of change in each coordinate direction. The resulting
linear approximation is called the <term>linearization</term> of
<m>f</m>, defined as follows.
</p>

<definition xml:id="def-skel-tp-linearization">
<statement>
<p>
The <term>linearization</term> of a differentiable function
<m>f(x,y)</m> at a point <m>(x_0,y_0)</m> is the function
<md>
<mrow>
L(x,y) \amp = f_x(x_0,y_0)(x - x_0)
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp \quad {} + f_y(x_0,y_0)(y - y_0)
</mrow>
<mrow xml:id="eq-skel-tp-linearization" number="yes">
\amp \quad {} + f(x_0,y_0).
</mrow>
</md>
The linearized function <m>L(x,y)</m> can be used as an approximation for
<m>f(x,y)</m> near <m>(x_0,y_0)</m>.
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>
What is the graph of <m>L</m>?
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,5);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,4.7) {the graph of $L$:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box for what the graph of the linearization is.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<example xml:id="ex-skel-tp-linearization">
<title>Linear Approximation of a Function</title>
<statement>
<p>
Consider the function <m>f(x,y) = -2x^2 - 2xy^3 - 2x</m>. Approximate
<m>f(x,y)</m> near the point
<m>P_0\left(-0.5,\, -0.5,\, f(-0.5,-0.5)\right)</m> with a linear
function <m>L(x,y)</m>.
</p>

<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,13);
\draw[gray!35] (0,5) -- (15,5);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,12.75) {workspace};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,4.7) {$L(x,y) =$};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace, with a last row for the linearization.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-tp-example-linearization">
<caption>
The graph of <m>z = -2x^2 - 2xy^3 - 2x</m> and its tangent plane
<m>z = L(x,y)</m> at <m>P_0</m>; compare with what you found. The view is close in on
<m>P_0</m>, where the plane hugs the surface, so that <m>L(x,y)</m>
approximates <m>f(x,y)</m>; farther out the surface curves away from the
plane. The arrow at <m>P_0</m> is the normal direction to the surface.
</caption>
<image width="85%">
<description>
<p>
A blue mesh surface, the graph of z equals negative 2 x squared minus 2 x
y cubed minus 2 x, seen close up around the point P 0. A flat dark red
patch of the tangent plane z equals L of x y cuts through the mesh,
touching it at the green point P 0: near P 0 the patch and the surface are
indistinguishable, while away from P 0 the surface curves down and away
from the flat patch. A long black arrow leaves P 0 at right angles to the
patch, showing the normal direction to the surface there. A legend below
the picture names the surface, the tangent plane, the point P 0, and the
normal direction.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\large]
\begin{axis}[
  width=10.26cm, height=10.01cm,
  view={-125}{25},
  % equal data units on all three axes, so the normal direction really is
  % drawn at a right angle to the tangent plane instead of being sheared
  % by different per-axis scales
  unit vector ratio=1 1 1,
  xmin=-0.96, xmax=-0.04, ymin=-0.96, ymax=-0.04, zmin=-0.46, zmax=0.66,
  xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, zlabel={$z$},
  xtick={-0.8,-0.6,-0.4,-0.2}, ytick={-0.8,-0.6,-0.4,-0.2},
  ztick={-0.4,-0.2,0,0.2,0.4,0.6},
  clip=false,
  legend style={
    at={(0.5,-0.08)}, anchor=north, font=\normalsize,
    draw=gray!50, fill=white,
    cells={anchor=west}, legend columns=2,
    row sep=1pt, column sep=8pt,
  },
]
% the surface z = -2x^2 - 2xy^3 - 2x (blue mesh)
\addplot3[surf, shader=flat, fill=blue!8, draw=blue!60,
          fill opacity=0.85, draw opacity=0.9,
          domain=-0.8:-0.2, y domain=-0.8:-0.2, samples=19,
          z buffer=sort, forget plot]
  {-2*x^2 - 2*x*y^3 - 2*x};
% the tangent plane patch z = L(x,y) at P0 (dark red)
\addplot3[surf, shader=flat, samples=2, domain=-0.72:-0.28,
          y domain=-0.72:-0.28,
          fill=red!50!black, fill opacity=0.55, draw=red!30!black,
          forget plot]
  {0.25*(x+0.5) + 0.75*(y+0.5) + 0.375};
% the normal direction at P0
\draw[-stealth, black, line width=1.1pt]
  (axis cs:-0.5,-0.5,0.375) -- (axis cs:-0.567,-0.702,0.644);
% the point P0
\addplot3[only marks, mark=*, mark size=1.9, color=green!60!black,
          forget plot]
  coordinates {(-0.5,-0.5,0.375)};
% legend
\addlegendimage{area legend, fill=blue!8, draw=blue!60}
\addlegendentry{$z=-2x^2-2xy^3-2x$}
\addlegendimage{area legend, fill=red!50!black, fill opacity=0.55,
                draw=red!30!black}
\addlegendentry{tangent plane $z=L(x,y)$}
\addlegendimage{only marks, mark=*, mark size=1.9, color=green!60!black}
\addlegendentry{$P_0\left(-\frac12,-\frac12,\frac38\right)$}
\addlegendimage{-stealth, black, line width=1.1pt}
\addlegendentry{normal direction at $P_0$}
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

</subsection>

</section>
